# Pi workflow reference

Env-specific facts at their current state, dated; if behavior seems changed,
verify against the source repository and update this file. Sandbox, mode and
approval questions: `~/.pi/agent/docs/sandbox.md`; sweep measurements and audit
baselines: the chezmoi repo's `docs/agents-audit-log.md`.

## Launch and ownership (last verified 2026-09-07)

- Launch through the managed `pi` zsh function: it runs the repository's
  lockfile-pinned CLI (`node_modules/.bin/pi`) on every platform — no Brew or
  metapac install — disables native tools and ambient extensions, then loads the
  managed workflow and Herdr when present. A binary run outside it bypasses that.
- Pi loads the applied copy under `~/.pi/agent/workflow`, not the repository
  source; `~/.pi/agent/node_modules` symlinks to the repository's pinned
  `node_modules`, which pi's documented "node_modules in a parent directory"
  lookup follows. A source edit reaches a session only through `chezmoi apply`.
- The repository's npm manifest/lockfile is the single source for the CLI, the
  extension dependencies and the worker SDK — one pinned unit (currently {{ index (fromJson (include "package.json")).dependencies "@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent" }}),
  so CLI/SDK skew cannot occur. Pi breaks extension APIs across 0.x point
  releases: upgrade by bumping that one pin, then `npm ci`, `npm run test:pi` and
  `chezmoi apply`; the npm dependency hook installs with lifecycle scripts off.
- Settings are merged by managed key; unrelated UI/trust preferences survive.
  Authentication and session files stay unmanaged — sign in through Pi's normal
  OpenAI subscription login, and do not import another harness's auth files.

