## Specialists and skills (last verified 2026-09-11)

- Specialists under `~/.pi/agent/agents` render from the shared roster, each with
  an explicit OpenAI model, tool list and mandatory child policy extension. Native
  builtin names cannot substitute for missing `workspace_*` tools; read-only
  specialists stay read-only even when the root enters execution. They are leaf
  roles: only `general-purpose` (every tool, the top worker tier) may launch
  children, to the same depth as Claude Code's built-in general-purpose agent.
- Use named `subagent` launches with an agent and a bounded task; the roster is
  in the tool description, so no `list` call is needed. Children run in the
  background, up to twenty sharing the root policy (Claude Code's default), so
  batch the independent launches that each repay their handoff into one turn.
- Children start fresh; `context: "fork"` branches this session's transcript into
  the child, as Codex's forked children do, for a second pass over material
  already read. They have no channel back: the pi-subagents intercom bridge is
  off in `extensions/subagent/config.json` (decision 2026-09-10).
- The upstream launch schema is narrowed from the same managed accepted-key
  definitions, so unsupported keys it advertises (`cwd`, `toolBudget`, …) are not
  offered; executor and description are retained, and launch/control enforcement
  is unchanged. Workflow scripts, task lists and chains are refused. Status (fleet
  or transcript view), stop, interrupt and non-recovering steer are enabled;
  project role overrides, revival and mutating management are not, and results
  arrive as notifications — `bg_wait` only when the turn depends on them
  (2026-09-16).
- Launched children's scope is off-limits to the parent (AGENTS.md).
- A child result "completed without making edits for an implementation task"
  is pi-subagents' completion guard, which accepts only a declared
  `mutationTools` call or a tracked-file diff; write roles declare their shell,
  edit and write names and read-only roles opt out. A run that still fails this
  way made no tool call beyond reads — read its report (2026-09-18).
- Transcript and status-line rendering follows the source repo's `docs/pi-design.md`;
  the undocumented surfaces it leans on are inventoried in `docs/pi-coupling.md`.
- Shared skills stay discoverable under `~/.agents/skills`; explicit skill
  loading is available through Pi. Inside a child, pi-subagents' tool allowlist
  may leave the direct `mcp__*` tools inactive; the `mcp` proxy is always
  allowlisted and reaches the same servers.

## Web search (last verified 2026-09-08, evening; unapplied)

- `web_search` is the pinned `pi-web-search` plugin: OpenAI's server-side
  `web_search` tool, on the same Codex Responses endpoint and subscription token
  as model calls, so it adds no endpoint, credential or network path beyond the
  provider's own — and sits in the same terms grey zone as the provider itself.
- Each search is one extra model request on the account's usage windows;
  `~/.pi/agent/web-search.json` (from `agents.pi.search_tier`) pins it to the small
  tier so the default model's quota is untouched.
- Roles with Claude's `WebSearch` get it; the plugin's Gemini-only `url_context`
  also registers and is in no role's list. Fetching stays with the Exa MCP tools
  and Playwright; OpenAI's search accepts extra `urls` to analyse
  alongside the query.

## MCP and code intelligence (last verified 2026-09-17)

- Context7 tools stay direct (e.g. `mcp__context7_query-docs`); Exa and Playwright
  stay behind the `mcp` proxy: `mcp({ search: "…" })` finds a tool in the cached
  catalog, `mcp({ describe: "<name>" })` shows its schema, `mcp({ tool: "<name>",
  args: { … } })` calls it; a server shown as not connected connects on first call.
- MCP servers other than Playwright run inside SRT; Node servers use
  `NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY=1` so fetch follows the sandbox proxy. If every call fails
  with `fetch failed`, check that setting, then the allowed domains. The browser
  exception and its permissions are documented in `sandbox.md`.
- Configuration is explicit; other harnesses' private config is not imported. MCP
  scripting, sampling and automatic OAuth discovery are disabled; the approval
  broker checks the resolved server/tool/arguments, not just the outer proxy call.
- No LSP here: diagnostics come from project toolchains via `workspace_bash`
  (`tsc --noEmit`, `go vet`, pyright, linters); rationale in the
  Decisions block of `docs/pi-implementation.md`.
- Claude/Codex proprietary plugins, cloud services and exact UI interactions are
  not supplied by these packages; keep the other harnesses for those.
